// NEGATIVE TEST -- `f64 as char` is refused (N4-04), and the DIRECTION is the
// point of a separate fixture.
//
// tests/reject/char_does_not_cast_to_bool.pd executes `char as bool`. It pins
// one cell of the matrix, and a matrix is not a predicate: the check that this
// row corrects was `castable(from) && castable(to)`, symmetric by construction,
// so a repair that got one direction right could get the other wrong and stay
// green. The refused pairs are char<->bool and char<->float, in both
// directions, and only a fixture per direction says so.
//
// MEASURED before the restriction: `3.7 as char` compiled and produced 3.
// Which character is three-tenths of the way past `\u{3}`? The specification
// answers nothing of the kind, so the cast is refused by name rather than
// truncating and hoping.
//
// `char` crosses to `i64` and back, because the code point IS the
// correspondence a character is defined by, and the way back is range-checked
// (tests/reject/char_from_non_scalar.pd).
fn main() {
    let c: char = 3.7 as char;
    print_int(c as i64);
}
